commentBody:
@Steve L

Puzzle editors are a vital and important part of puzzle creation and publishing, and what a sorry world we would live in if puzzle authors were given free rein to use their own, original clues, verbatim ac litteratim.  So please stop calling them venal, hyena-brained weenies.  Just cut it right out.

That said, I'm in the mood for a dog in heat.

@Andrew

I very much agree.  While the easiest crosses were pretty easy, the hardest crosses were virtually impossible or anyone who doesn't  know Canary Island geography, or gymnasts.

Moreover, it demonstrates the absolute mess English spelling is.

@Al  in Pittsburgh

ROLAIDS had me stumped. I had to rely on crosses, exclusively, to get it. RAITA was a gimme - although I think of it more as a condiment than an actual dish: a small serving of it always accompanies biryani at Warsaw's Indian restaurants, and I'm a scuker for a lamb biryani.

English spelling is a lawless mess 🤣

@CrispyShot

😀  Consider me chided.

I do admit that I seem to recoil at retaining any knowledge of  gymnastics or figure skating.

@john ezra

I find it interesting that they went to the trouble to make different labels for the same product.  I'm going to look at the canned legumes more closely next time I grocery shop.

Coincidentally, I bought a can of  field peas last week and noticed the calories per serving had decreased from the last can I bought.

@Jim
Sneaky. More servings per can? Or less total weight with the same number of servings per can? I doubt they've magically reduced the calories per petit pois.

Shrink-flation. (Sigh.)

@Francis

You nailed it!

JESUS

@Emma

To address the Satan clue in the column would invariably address its inadequacy / inaptness / awkwardness.

As SBK implied, the columnists almost NEVER criticize the puzzle in question.  If I were in their job I would likely not be allowed to either.  So better to just ignore the really weak spots...

@Francis

Now you know how it feels for me when a baseball player crosses with an American town, or a SNL alum with a Florida college 🤣.

Las PALMAS and Suni LEE were both gimmes - trivia in these puzzles rarely is, so I was quite surprised.

@Andrzej

😆  Oh, I fully acknowledge that if we were competing here, and I very much hope we're not, that the deck is stacked in my favor to an embarrassing degree.

How in the world would anyone know anything about the Canary Islands without having been to the Canary Islands?  I doubt that I've given them a moments thought in years, other than maybe I think their name is pretty.

@Francis

I needed the column to understand the theme though.

And yes, WEENIE seemed a bit ridiculous, didn't it.

@Barry Ancona
Tch. Two minutes in the penalty box for excessive literalism. 
It's clear that Francis meant 'without some direct personal connection or experience of the Canaries.'

@Eric Hougland

Why did you think the SATAN clue was cute? It seemed pretty random to me (and Francis). Can just any phrase with "heII" in it be used to clue the guy?

Ditto on SPACE NEEDLE! My wife and I visited Seattle and the mountain was visible from almost anywhere in the city. An amazing sight it was, too (especially for people from a flat country like Poland).

